From 1efcecac5bc29a89d7fbc15e269a2e9f993d325d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Peripheral Pretender setup <pp-setup@localhost>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 11:28:40 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] hw/timer: TCC recoverable and non-recoverable faults (U2213)

The TCC's faults, as a PIC32CM MC00 does them (firmware/hello
APP=tccprobe8 and tccprobe9, checked by tccfault). Recoverable faults A
and B take the new "mc-event-in" inputs MC0 and MC1 (the EVSYS users),
which need EVCTRL.MCEIx as well as FCTRLx.SRC (ENABLE or INVERT),
through the FILTERVAL filter and two cycles of synchronization. QUAL
ignores a fault while its channel's waveform is low; BLANK ignores one
for BLANKVAL (x64 with BLANKPRESC) GCLK_TCC cycles after the selected
waveform edge, or after each cycle start but the first. While a fault
acts its channel is clamped low ahead of the output matrix (STATUS.CMPx
still shows the waveform), to the end of the cycle with KEEP; RESTART
retriggers the counter, HALT HW holds it while the fault lasts and SW
until software clears STATUS.FAULTx, CAPTURE CAPT captures COUNT into a
CPTENx channel. STATUS.FAULTxIN and FAULTx, INTFLAG.FAULTx.

Non-recoverable faults (EV0/EV1 with the FAULT action, filtered by
DRVCTRL.FILTERVALn, or FCTRLx.HALT = NR) hold the counter and put the
outputs in the DRVCTRL non-recoverable state until software clears
STATUS.FAULTx with the input gone; counting goes on from where it
stopped. STATUS.FAULTx are write-one-to-clear.

A retrigger while running no longer zeroes STATUS.CMPx until the next
tick: silicon's outputs go on without a glitch.

qtest: clamp, keep, qualification, restart, halt, blanking and filter,
capture, non-recoverable faults.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
